  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday morning through next Thursday)\nIssued at 231 PM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nThe long term period will begin with a vertically stacked low \npressure system lingering near the New England Coast and a high \npressie mid-level ridge extending through the central CONUS. North\nand central Georgia will be positioned underneath northwesterly \nflow between these two features, which will continue to bring dry,\nCanadian continental air into the region. This dry air, which \nwill limit dewpoints to the 40s and low 50s and precipitable water\nvalues to 0.4-0.7 inches, combined with subsidence underneath \nlingering surface high pressure will inhibit rain chances through \nat least mid- week. On Monday and Tuesday, the low pressure system\nwill gradually weaken and move away to the northeast as the ridge\nadvances eastward. High temperatures on Sunday will be near to \njust above climatological normals, in the mid 70s to low 80s in \nnorth Georgia and low to mid 80s in central Georgia. Very dry air \nwill remain under the ridge, though the airmass will moderate and \ntemperatures will steadily climb each day. Low temperatures on \nMonday and Tuesday will remain brisk and seasonable, in the upper \n40s in far northeast Georgia and 50s elsewhere across the area. In\ncontrast, highs by Tuesday afternoon will be in the mid to upper \n80s across the majority of north Georgia and low 90s in central \nGeorgia, which will be about 6-10 degrees above climatological \nnormals. With several days of dry weather and sunny skies, minimum\nrelative humidity values will need to be monitored on Monday and \nTuesday afternoons, as they could approach 25% in central Georgia \nduring peak heating each of those days.\n\nOn Wednesday, the axis of the ridge will move east of Georgia as \nthe associated surface high moves off the Carolina Coast. Moisture\nwill gradually begin to increase as the flow pattern shifts to \nsouthwesterly. At this point, the tug-of-war between fall and \nsummer will be pulled back in the favor of \"summer\" as highs \nremain in the mid 80s to low 90s and highs return to the 60s and \nlow 70s late in the week. A low pressure system moving through the\nGreat Plains will extend a cold front southward, which is \nanticipated to bring a chance of precipitation back to portions of\nthe forecast area late in the week. Mid-range model guidance \nremains highly inconsistent with the handling of the low, with \nsome solutions indicating a weakening of the low pressure system \nas it is quickly pulled to the northeast while others indicate the\nsystem failing to be lifted northeast by the jet stream to the \nnorth and instead meandering across the southern Great Plains and \nOzarks region. Which solution materializes will determine the \nchances and timing of any precipitation that occurs over Georgia \nand will warrant continued monitoring.",
